    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "An assessment of matrix permeability variations is necessary to address fluid flow problems in fractured porous rock. This study uses a field minipermeameter (probe permeameter) to investigate the 2 dimensional permeability structure of a single, small fault in porous sandstone. Gridded permeability data were collected over a 80 cm2 area at different sample spacings ranging from 1x0.5 cm to 5x5 cm. Data collected at samples localities identified as undeformed matrix are used to estimate the pre-fault matrix permeability distribution. The estimated restored permeabilities are used in conjunction with the mea- sured permeabilities to calculate percent change be- tween the deformed and undeformed states. Matrix rock permeability reductions in the vicinity of secondary structures are calculated as high as -50%, with some as high as -75%. The fault core shows average permeability reductions of -100%, though, this is considered a minimum estimate of the true reduction as the fault rock permeabilities are likely below the detection limit of the instrument.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
